# Adelic Riemann–Roch from existence of the Stichtenoth genus

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.exists_genus_riemannIndex_of_stichtenothGenusExists`
(`54cdae2a-d941-5bef-9844-e8cf4f94900d`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `66288c62-f31d-5d34-8e8a-a6eac684f3f2`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra satisfying `IsCurveOver K F`, that is: every
nonzero $f \in F$ has a divisor whose coefficient at each place $v$ is $v.\mathrm{ord}\, f$ and
whose degree is $0$; each place has residue field finite over $K$; and $\Omega_{F/K}$ is free of
rank one over $F$. Assume `StichtenothGenusExists K F`: there is at least one place of $F/K$, the
Riemann–Roch space $L(0)$ is finite-dimensional over $K$, and there are $\gamma_0 \in \mathbb{Z}$
and a divisor $D_0$ with $L(D_0)$ finite-dimensional, $\deg D_0 - \ell(D_0) = \gamma_0 - 1$, and
$\deg D - \ell(D) \le \gamma_0 - 1$ for every divisor $D$. Here divisors are finitely supported
$\mathbb{Z}$-valued functions on places, $\deg$ is the sum of coefficients weighted by the residue
degrees, and $\ell(D) = \dim_K L(D)$. The conclusion asserts the existence of an integer $\gamma$
such that for every divisor $D$: first, the quotient of the adele space $\mathbb{A} = \sup_E
\{\alpha \mid v(\alpha_v) \le \exp(E_v) \text{ for all } v\}$ inside $\prod_v F$ by the submodule
generated by $\{\alpha \in \mathbb{A} \mid v(\alpha_v) \le \exp(D_v)\}$ together with the image of
the diagonal map $F \to \prod_v F$ is a finite $K$-module; and second, its $K$-dimension $i(D)$
satisfies $i(D) = \ell(D) - (\deg D + 1 - \gamma)$ in $\mathbb{Z}$. The integer $\gamma$ is produced
existentially and is not asserted to equal $\gamma_0$.

This is the adelic form of the Riemann–Roch theorem, $\ell(D) - i(D) = \deg D + 1 - \gamma$, with
$\gamma$ the Stichtenoth genus defined by the maximality property in the hypothesis; the index of
specialty is computed as the $K$-dimension of the adele class group attached to $D$. It is used to
obtain the same conclusion from the curve axioms alone and to extract the uniform bound $\deg D + 1
- \ell(D) \le \gamma$.

